1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,700
If you heat titanium dioxide with lithium
carbonate, you will form a battery material.

2
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:08,500
X-rays let us watch each substance
rise and fall in real time.

3
00:00:09,100 --> 00:00:13,100
Our question, can an AI agent work out
the reaction steps from curves like these?

4
00:00:13,700 --> 00:00:17,700
Does its set of candidate pathways include
one that correctly forecasts a new experiment,

5
00:00:18,100 --> 00:00:21,600
and can we pick that one only
using the data it was fitted to?

6
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:28,900
We fit at 700 and 725 degrees and forecast the
750 degree experiment, which no model has seen.

7
00:00:31,033 --> 00:00:36,433
NernstWork splits the job. The AI reads the
data and the notes from the literature and

8
00:00:36,433 --> 00:00:41,333
proposes the building blocks, which substances,
which steps, and how fast each might run,

9
00:00:41,533 --> 00:00:46,533
each with a reason. Code then checks that
every combination conserves atoms and fits

10
00:00:46,533 --> 00:00:52,533
them all the same way, using only the 700
and 725 degree data. The picks are locked

11
00:00:52,533 --> 00:00:57,833
in before we load 750. Finally, the AI tells
us what the data can't settle and which

12
00:00:57,833 --> 00:00:58,533
experiment would.

13
00:01:00,033 --> 00:01:07,233
First, what code alone would do? It tries
153 combinations of a fixed menu of steps and

14
00:01:07,233 --> 00:01:13,533
picks the best fit. That pick ranks 11th at
forecasting 750 degrees. Now the AI's route.

15
00:01:14,033 --> 00:01:20,233
It adds its own steps, code fits every combination,
and the same best fit rule picks one. That one

16
00:01:20,233 --> 00:01:25,733
ranks 4th, but the agent also named the second
plausible model before seeing 750, and that

17
00:01:25,933 --> 00:01:26,433
one ranks first.

18
00:01:27,833 --> 00:01:32,949
Each gray line is one reference model. On
the left is its ranking by how well it fit

19
00:01:32,949 --> 00:01:37,333
the data we had. On its right is its
ranking at forecasting 750 degrees.

20
00:01:38,333 --> 00:01:41,233
As you can see, the best fit
is not the best forecaster.

21
00:01:42,733 --> 00:01:48,233
Across four AI runs, two Claude and two
Gemini, every set of candidates held a

22
00:01:48,233 --> 00:01:52,333
top-four forecaster, though we only
know that after seeing the 750 degree

23
00:01:52,333 --> 00:01:58,633
results. Picking by the best fit chose
anywhere from fourth to 36th best, so

24
00:01:58,633 --> 00:02:03,033
the AI shrinks hundreds of possibilities
to a short list and choosing among them

25
00:02:03,033 --> 00:02:03,833
takes an experiment.

26
00:02:05,100 --> 00:02:07,800
So, the agent suggests what to measure next.

27
00:02:08,200 --> 00:02:11,400
The experiment where its candidate
explanations would disagree the most.

28
00:02:12,100 --> 00:02:15,800
Its top pick is to halve the
lithium carbonate at 725 degrees.

29
00:02:16,400 --> 00:02:20,500
That would test its rival idea that forming
the battery material is linked to a second

30
00:02:20,500 --> 00:02:23,400
form of titanium dioxide, rutile forming.

31
00:02:24,100 --> 00:02:28,100
By two minutes, the two explanations
differ by far more than measurement noise.

32
00:02:28,100 --> 00:02:32,200
Two of its leading models can't be
told apart, and the agent says so.

33
00:02:33,067 --> 00:02:38,667
Did the AI's candidates include a model that
correctly forecasts the 750-degree experiment

34
00:02:38,667 --> 00:02:41,267
having only seen 700 and 725?

35
00:02:41,867 --> 00:02:43,067
Yes, every time.

36
00:02:43,567 --> 00:02:47,167
Can we pick that model using
only the 700 and 725 data?

37
00:02:47,667 --> 00:02:48,367
Not reliably.

38
00:02:49,067 --> 00:02:52,667
The chemistry agreed across models and
the agent said what it couldn't settle.

39
00:02:53,367 --> 00:02:57,967
One illustration of why this matters is that
a study we found through Valency made titanium

40
00:02:57,967 --> 00:03:01,167
dioxide catalysts at 700 and 900 degrees.

41
00:03:01,167 --> 00:03:06,567
Two different crystal forms and a rutile one
worked better for turning nitrate into ammonia.

42
00:03:07,067 --> 00:03:08,767
That's one example, not a result.

43
00:03:09,467 --> 00:03:11,467
The AI is useful for proposing pathways.

44
00:03:12,067 --> 00:03:14,367
Choosing among them still
takes a targeted experiment.

45
00:03:15,467 --> 00:03:20,367
Through NernstWork, we've shown that an AI
agent can narrow a large space of possible

46
00:03:20,367 --> 00:03:25,367
reaction pathways in solid-state synthesis
down to a short list of good candidates and

47
00:03:25,367 --> 00:03:26,567
suggest what to measure next.

48
00:03:27,167 --> 00:03:32,267
The implication is more targeted experiments
in physical labs, experiments chosen because

49
00:03:32,267 --> 00:03:35,767
they tell rival explanations
apart rather than tried blindly.

50
00:03:36,267 --> 00:03:38,867
It points to a better way
to design experiments.
